Propagate uncertainty with sensitivities and covariance (@met-sensitivity: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ", #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #source "../sources/GUM-Multivariate.lingua#r_1S5554FGCGA5E8GKAM6C9KMJKK", #metrology "Coverage-and-degrees-of-freedom.lingua#r_5Q2X2FWBE479BM2WERZQNG9PCV") { r_0EF35BB106S2F2ATJBXBR49DFR
**Read:** [GUM — JCGM 100:2008 and the 2026 nonlinearity amendment](../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ), the combined-uncertainty treatment for correlated inputs; [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), shared effects and statistical models; [Several output quantities — JCGM 102](../sources/GUM-Multivariate.lingua#r_1S5554FGCGA5E8GKAM6C9KMJKK), the covariance formulation.

For a sufficiently smooth scalar model y = f(x₁,...,x_n), first-order propagation gives u²(y) ≈ Σ_i Σ_j c_i c_j cov(x_i,x_j), with c_i = ∂f/∂x_i evaluated at the input estimates. Each term must have output-unit squared. Under independence, covariance terms vanish, leaving the familiar sum of squared standard-uncertainty contributions.

For a two-input difference y = x₁ − x₂, u²(y) = u₁² + u₂² − 2cov(x₁,x₂) in the exact linear model. With u₁ = u₂ = 1 unit and correlation 0.8, u(y) = √0.4, about 0.632 units. The sum instead has standard uncertainty √3.6, about 1.897 units. A shared contribution can cancel in one result and reinforce another.

Do not infer independence merely because observations came from different files, operators, or dates. Common reference standards, coefficient fits, environmental corrections, conservation constraints, and shared preparation can create dependence. Conversely, a shared label does not determine the numerical correlation without a model.

**Work:** derive sensitivities for a corrected length and for a ratio. Check them by finite perturbations over the relevant input ranges using [Small changes, derivatives, and first-order approximations](../../quantitative-literacy/models/Small-changes-and-derivatives.lingua#r_4T51QH8VRCNNMY5WD30VJS5P52) and [Sensitivity, cancellation, dependencies, and conditioning](../../quantitative-literacy/models/Sensitivity-and-conditioning.lingua#r_43NVV3KJX9W9R026T1NKCYMP3T). Reproduce the correlated sum and difference results and compare a model that explicitly includes the common effect with one using a covariance matrix.

A covariance matrix must be symmetric and positive semidefinite. For two inputs, |cov| ≤ u₁u₂; violating this is a mathematical inconsistency, not an unusual physical case. Retain a singular but valid covariance when a constraint makes outputs exactly dependent. Local linear propagation must also be checked against the nonlinear behavior studied next.

